WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.522
<v Examiner>Pedigree charts.

2
00:00:02.522 --> 00:00:03.287
<v Examiner>Five marks.

3
00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:07.901
<v Examiner>Explain how you work out the mode of inheritance from a pedigree chart,

4
00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:12.593
<v Examiner>how you assign genotypes, and how you find the chance of another affected child.

5
00:00:12.593 --> 00:00:15.467
<v Examiner>Use an autosomal dominant family as your example:

6
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:18.048
<v Examiner>an affected father and an unaffected mother,

7
00:00:18.048 --> 00:00:20.804
<v Examiner>who have both affected and unaffected children.

8
00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:24.700
In her own words, printed on six of her quiz questions:

9
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.268
pedigree charts will be tested in Test 3.

10
00:00:27.268 --> 00:00:31.152
So this one is close to certain, either written out like this,

11
00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:33.094
or as a chart you have to read.

12
00:00:33.544 --> 00:00:36.616
The hook is three questions, from Emma's slides.

13
00:00:36.616 --> 00:00:38.600
One: does it skip a generation?

14
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.480
Two: is there a difference between the sexes?

15
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.104
Three: who are the homozygous recessives?

16
00:00:44.104 --> 00:00:45.512
Then a Punnett square.

17
00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:47.518
Step one, read the chart.

18
00:00:47.518 --> 00:00:48.639
Squares are males.

19
00:00:48.639 --> 00:00:49.884
Circles are females.

20
00:00:49.884 --> 00:00:51.254
Shaded means affected.

21
00:00:51.254 --> 00:00:53.681
Half shaded, or a dot, means a carrier.

22
00:00:53.681 --> 00:00:56.794
A line through a symbol means the person has died.

23
00:00:56.794 --> 00:00:59.845
The generations are Roman numerals down the side,

24
00:00:59.845 --> 00:01:02.770
and the people are numbered from left to right.

25
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:07.253
And when you check the generations, leave out the people who married in.

26
00:01:07.703 --> 00:01:09.822
Step two, dominant or recessive?

27
00:01:09.822 --> 00:01:14.590
If the trait appears in every generation and never skips, it's dominant.

28
00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.305
If it skips a generation, it's recessive.

29
00:01:17.305 --> 00:01:22.337
The giveaway for recessive is an affected child with two unaffected parents.

30
00:01:22.787 --> 00:01:25.090
Step three, autosomal or X-linked?

31
00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.476
Count the affected males and the affected females.

32
00:01:28.476 --> 00:01:30.033
About equal: autosomal.

33
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:32.133
A clear difference: sex-linked.

34
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:37.212
And one quick test: if a father passes it to his son, it can't be X-linked,

35
00:01:37.212 --> 00:01:40.259
because a son gets his father's Y, not his X.

36
00:01:41.709 --> 00:01:44.955
The X-linked rules, in case the chart is lopsided.

37
00:01:44.955 --> 00:01:48.784
In X-linked recessive, fathers can't pass it to their sons,

38
00:01:48.784 --> 00:01:52.159
and an affected mother passes it to all of her sons.

39
00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:57.027
In X-linked dominant, an affected father passes it to all of his daughters.

40
00:01:57.477 --> 00:01:58.786
Step four, genotypes.

41
00:01:58.786 --> 00:02:02.401
Label the homozygous recessives first: little b, little b.

42
00:02:02.401 --> 00:02:05.330
Everyone else has at least one dominant allele,

43
00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:09.818
and you work out their second one from their parents and their children.

44
00:02:09.818 --> 00:02:13.496
In her example, the affected father must be big B little b,

45
00:02:13.496 --> 00:02:17.423
because some of his children are unaffected, little b little b,

46
00:02:17.423 --> 00:02:19.978
and each of them got a little b from him.

47
00:02:19.978 --> 00:02:22.659
The unaffected mother is little b little b.

48
00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:24.511
Step five, the chance.

49
00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:26.868
Draw a Punnett square of the parents.

50
00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:29.863
Big B little b, crossed with little b little b.

51
00:02:29.863 --> 00:02:34.069
Two boxes are big B little b, and two boxes are little b little b.

52
00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:37.893
So each child has a fifty per cent chance of being affected.

53
00:02:39.343 --> 00:02:40.003
The traps.

54
00:02:40.003 --> 00:02:44.759
Say how you can tell: skips or not, males versus females, father to son.

55
00:02:44.759 --> 00:02:48.062
The "how can you tell" part carries its own marks.

56
00:02:48.062 --> 00:02:50.770
Second: father to son rules out X-linked.

57
00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.818
Third: the chance is per child.

58
00:02:52.818 --> 00:02:57.177
Fifty per cent for every pregnancy, not one in every two children.

59
00:02:57.627 --> 00:02:58.178
<v Examiner>Once more.

60
00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:01.979
<v Examiner>How do you read a pedigree, assign the genotypes, and get the chance?

61
00:03:04.429 --> 00:03:08.242
Squares are males, circles are females, shaded is affected.

62
00:03:08.242 --> 00:03:11.215
In every generation, never skipping: dominant.

63
00:03:11.215 --> 00:03:13.153
Skips a generation: recessive.

64
00:03:13.153 --> 00:03:18.258
Males and females about equal: autosomal, and father to son rules out X-linked.

65
00:03:18.258 --> 00:03:20.714
Label the homozygous recessives first,

66
00:03:20.714 --> 00:03:24.591
then work out everyone else from their parents and children:

67
00:03:24.591 --> 00:03:29.438
the affected father is big B little b, and the mother is little b little b.

68
00:03:29.438 --> 00:03:33.896
The Punnett square gives two big B little b to two little b little b:

69
00:03:33.896 --> 00:03:36.417
a fifty per cent chance for each child.
